        "id": "ch1_3_8-1",
        "text": "24\nChapter 1 I Mastering the Terminal Window\nTo mount a USB drive, follow these steps:\n1.I Display the disk list using the lsblk command.\n2. Create a new directory to be mounted (this is where you will access the\nUSB stick drive).\n3.I Mount the USB drive using the mount command.\nNow, to eject the USB drive, use the umount command to unmount the directory:\nroot@kali-laptop-hp:~# umount /mnt/usb\nManaging Text Files in Kali Linux\nKnowing how to handle files in Kali Linux is something that you\u2019ll often encounter\nduring your engagements. In this section, you will learn about the most common\ncommands that you can use to get the job done.\nThere are many ways to display a text file quickly on the terminal window.\n90 percent of the time, I use the cat command for this purpose. What if you\nwant to display a large text file (e.g., a password\u2019s dictionary file)? Then you\nhave three choices: the head , tail , and more and less commands. It is important\nto note that you can use the grep command to filter out the results that you\u2019re\nFigure 1.8: Mount Using the Command Line\nFigure 1.7: USB Mount\nChapter 1 I Mastering the Terminal Window\n25\nlooking for. For example, to identify the word gus123 inside the rockyou.txt\ndictionary file, you can do the following:\nroot@kali:/usr/share/wordlists# cat rockyou.txt | grep gus123\ngus123\nangus123\ngus12345\n[...]\nThe head command will display 10 lines in a text file starting from the top, and\nyou can specify how many lines you want to display by adding the -n option:\n$head -n [i] [file name]\nroot@kali:/usr/share/wordlists# head -n 7 rockyou.txt\n123456\n12345\n123456789\npassword\niloveyou\nprincess\n1234567\nThe tail command will display the last 10 lines in a file, and you can specify\nthe number of lines as well using the -n switch:",
